      "id": 1,
      "text": "Richmond was part of Westerly from 1669 until 1747, a period when Rhode Island, Connecticut, and Massachusetts all claimed the territory.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Richmond%2C_Rhode_Island",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "In 1665 King Charles II renamed the disputed area King's County, which later became Washington County during the Revolution.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Richmond%2C_Rhode_Island",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Richmond’s textile heritage began with a mill in Hope Valley in 1810, followed by Wyoming in 1814 and the Carolina Mill in 1842.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.richmondrihistoricalsoc.org/copy-of-a-brief-history-of-the-town",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The town sits where three rivers meet, a landscape that the Narragansett people once called home.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.richmondrihistoricalsoc.org/copy-of-a-brief-history-of-the-town",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Since 1970, Richmond has hosted Rhode Island’s largest fair, the Washington County Fair, drawing visitors from across the state.",
